- id: '2097977165274923015'
  text: 'Japanese shrine. Realistic materials. Cinematic motion—all generated with
    GPT-6 Astra. 🌿⛩️


    Made by @PixVerse. Available on PixVerse and in ChatGPT via the PixVerse Plugin.


    Prompt ↓

    Create an original Japanese torii shrine scene using PixVerse and Blender. Complete
    these steps: Create the concept: Use PixVerse to generate a photoreal reference
    image. Lock the torii design, materials, lighting, and environment for consistency.
    Build the 3D scene: Create an editable textured model in Blender. Clean the topology,
    unwrap the UVs, and add suitable animation controls. Add movement: Include foreground
    leaves that partially obscure the wide shots, gently moving foliage, and birds
    flying visibly across the scene. Animate the cameras: Create a 12-second sequence
    with three shots: an arcing approach, a lateral detail shot, and a rising pullback.
    Keep movement smooth and framing clear. Render in Blender: Export a native camera
    video and a separate neutral gray clay reference video using the same animation.
    Save them beside the Blender project. Generate the photoreal film: Use PixVerse
    Seedance with the gray video for camera and action reference and the unchanged
    photoreal image for appearance. Generate a 1080p, 16:9 video with only natural
    environmental sounds—wind, rustling leaves, and bird wings. No music or voices.
    Check and deliver: Review every shot for consistent design, photoreal textures,
    visible birds, smooth motion, and correct audio. Fix issues within the approved
    budget and document remaining limitations. Save the editable Blender project,
    videos, reference image, and a brief production assessment in the same folder.
    Key points: Never use the colored low-poly video as the Seedance reference. Check
    the entire film, especially the final wide shot.'
