- id: '2098103823877148906'
  text: 'I asked GPT-6 to find me the best free-to-use scripts on the Internet.


    A few hours later, it built the most comprehensive database.


    Here''s the story:


    Last night, I asked GPT-6 to find me ~100 scripts that are in the public domain,
    free to use, or under a Creative Commons license.


    This morning, I woke up to a fully built website with filters, search, rankings,
    cost estimates, scripts, etc., for 111 scripts.


    It generates a comprehensive PDF for each script explaining how to build the entire
    film using AI, or just a short trailer if you wish.


    You can pick any of them. It will connect via the Higgsfield plugin and then start
    generating characters, locations, props, visual style, mood boards, etc.


    Then it automatically starts generating the movie, clip by clip, editing it, finding
    appropriate music, adding sound effects, and putting it all together. It also
    corrects itself if something is off.


    Every image or video generation uses my prompting style and knowledge, which I
    previously trained, and it uses specific models for each task. For example, the
    characters are generated with Higgsfield''s Soul 2.0. I just love how random-looking
    they are in this AI landscape where everything starts to look the same.


    This whole process left me a bit speechless, considering it all started with just
    ONE (massive) prompt last night.


    As for costs, it accounted for ~50% of my weekly GPT-6 usage on the $200 sub.


    So far, I did a 3-min and a 6-min short film, and a few trailers.


    Check out one of the trailers below that I generated for the app''s highest-ranked
    script.


    The whole film was estimated to cost $14,176 for 40 minutes of runtime.


    I just won''t pull the trigger on it yet, but it''s really fun to explore its
    universe.


    PS: It updates itself every night with new scripts.'
